# UI Safe Area — `SafeArea`

Every player-facing screen must keep interactive content inside the device safe
area. The reusable pieces live in the shared screen assembly
(`MonkeyTilt.Shared.Screen`, namespace `MonkeyTilt.Shared`):

- `SafeArea` — a `MonoBehaviour` that drives its own `RectTransform` from the
  device safe area. Drop it on a full-stretch child of any Canvas.
- `SafeArea.CreateRoot(Transform parent)` — creates a full-stretch child
  carrying `SafeArea`; parent interactive content under the returned rect.

## The layout contract

`Canvas → [full-bleed background] + [SafeArea → interactive content]`

- **Interactive content** (buttons, text, panels, edge-anchored HUD chrome)
  goes **inside** the safe-area root.
- **Full-bleed visuals** (dim/blur backdrops, full-screen modal scrims,
  decorative effects) stay on the parent **before** the safe-area child, so
  they still cover notches and the home indicator. Never wrap a full-screen
  scrim in the safe area — it would letterbox under the notch.

## Pattern for a new screen (prefab-authored)

Author the hierarchy on the prefab in Unity MCP/Editor. The view script only
holds refs — it does **not** build panels in `Awake`.

```csharp
// On the screen prefab (Inspector wiring):
// Canvas
//   ├── Backdrop          (full-bleed dim/blur — outside safe area)
//   └── SafeAreaRoot      (child with SafeArea component)
//         └── ContentRoot ([SerializeField] _contentRoot — buttons, text, panels)

[SerializeField] private RectTransform _contentRoot; // safe-area child; hierarchy authored on prefab

private void Awake()
{
    WireButtons(); // callbacks only — no Build* / Create*Panel methods
}
```

When bootstrapping a screen that is not yet prefab-migrated,
`SafeArea.CreateRoot` may run once at runtime to add the safe-area wrapper —
but **content under it must still come from the prefab**, not from procedural
`AddComponent` layout code. See the `no-code-built-ui` rule.

## WebGL: the browser bridge

`Screen.safeArea` is **not implemented on the Web platform** — it always
returns the full canvas. `SafeArea` bridges the browser's CSS
`env(safe-area-inset-*)` values in via the `Plugins/WebGL/WebSafeArea.jslib`
that ships inside that same assembly's folder (extern
`MonkeyTiltGetSafeAreaInsets`). This requires **`viewport-fit=cover`**
in the hosting page's viewport meta tag, otherwise the insets are always 0.
Don't add a second jslib per project — the shared one is the bridge.

## Rules

- New screens never anchor interactive elements directly to the raw canvas —
  go through a safe-area root (`SafeArea` component on a prefab child, or
  `SafeArea.CreateRoot` in legacy code-built screens).
- `SafeArea` polls in `Update` (no Unity safe-area-changed event exists) and
  allocates nothing — don't "optimize" it into per-frame work or add
  allocations.
- Dev/debug overlays are exempt.
